text:
Classic read-modify-write race. Two requests read same value, both write same increment.

**Root cause:**
```js
// BROKEN — read then write = race
const { rows } = await db.query('SELECT count FROM counters WHERE id = $1', [id]);
const newVal = rows[0].count + 1;
await db.query('UPDATE counters SET count = $1 WHERE id = $2', [newVal, id]);
```

**Fix 1 — Atomic update + RETURNING (best):**
```js
const { rows } = await db.query(
  'UPDATE counters SET count = count + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING count',
  [id]
);
return rows[0].count; // guaranteed unique per caller
```
Single statement. DB handle concurrency. No app-level lock needed.

---

**Fix 2 — Need conditional logic? Use transaction + row lock:**
```js
await db.query('BEGIN');
const { rows } = await db.query(
  'SELECT count FROM counters WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE', // locks row
  [id]
);
const newVal = rows[0].count + 1;
await db.query('UPDATE counters SET count = $1 WHERE id = $2', [newVal, id]);
await db.query('COMMIT');
return newVal;
```
`FOR UPDATE` block other transactions until lock release.

---

**Fix 3 — Counter not exist yet? Use upsert:**
```js
const { rows } = await db.query(`
  INSERT INTO counters (id, count) VALUES ($1, 1)
  ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET count = counters.count + 1
  RETURNING count
`, [id]);
return rows[0].count;
```

---

**Pick:**
| Situation | Use |
|-----------|-----|
| Simple increment | Fix 1 |
| Complex logic in transaction | Fix 2 |
| Counter may not exist | Fix 3 |

Fix 1 cover 90% of cases. One round-trip, zero race.
